Retry the worker entry when the database is lost

`CinderCleanableObject.set_workers` (cinder/objects/cleanable.py) wraps the
RPC methods that work on a cleanable resource, among them `create_volume` of
the scheduler and of the volume manager. Before the method runs,
`set_worker` reads the resource's row in the workers table and claims it.
When the database is gone at that moment, `db.worker_get` raises
`DBConnectionError`, the method never runs, and oslo.messaging has consumed
the message already. No flow exists whose revert could end the operation,
so a volume create stays in `creating`: no user can delete it, and only a
cleanup of the workers table (a restart of the service, or the workers
cleanup API) ends it. The patch that sets a volume to error when its create
loses the database does not reach this path, because its retried
`error_out` belongs to the flow.

Random chaos found this in the e2e-chaos-random job of pull request #1598
(run 41807396659): a volume create sent during `rabbitmq-pod-failure`
blocked in the API's cast until the broker came back, and the cast reached
cinder-scheduler during the `mariadb-pod-failure` that followed. dizzy's
teardown failed on the volume with a 400 and exited 1. A targeted replay on
a kind plane reproduced it: a create sent 10 s into a RabbitMQ pod failure
was answered after 138 s, the scheduler took the message 9 s into a MariaDB
pod failure and logged the `DBConnectionError` of `worker_get`, and the
volume was still `creating` seven minutes after both faults lifted.

`set_worker` now retries a `DBConnectionError` with waits that add up to
254 seconds, the budget of `error_out`'s save, which outlasts a database
failover. The retry is safe: until an attempt succeeds, `self.worker` stays
unset, so each attempt reads the row again and creates or claims it from
there. A database that stays away longer still fails the call, as before.

Tests: no upstream test pins a lost database in `set_worker`; the existing
tests mock the database calls and keep their behaviour. The patch adds three
tests to cinder/tests/unit/objects/test_cleanable.py, and all three fail
without it.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_cinder.sh asserts in the built image
that `set_worker` reads again after a lost database and then creates the
worker entry.

Applies to cinder 28.0.0 (2026.1).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

diff --git a/cinder/objects/cleanable.py b/cinder/objects/cleanable.py
index 10bf0f2..7bd0bdc 100644
--- a/cinder/objects/cleanable.py
+++ b/cinder/objects/cleanable.py
@@ -17,12 +17,14 @@ import collections.abc as collections
 import inspect
 
 import decorator
+from oslo_db import exception as db_exc
 from oslo_utils import versionutils
 
 from cinder import db
 from cinder import exception
 from cinder.objects import base
 from cinder import service
+from cinder import utils
 from cinder.volume import rpcapi as vol_rpcapi
 
 
@@ -120,6 +122,13 @@ class CinderCleanableObject(base.CinderPersistentObject):
                     pass
         return entry_in_db
 
+    # set_workers calls this before the decorated method runs, so a lost
+    # database here drops the RPC call with nothing left to end the
+    # operation: a volume create stays in 'creating'. The retries wait
+    # 254 seconds in all, as error_out's save does
+    # (cinder/volume/flows/common.py), which outlasts a database failover.
+    @utils.retry(db_exc.DBConnectionError, interval=2, retries=8,
+                 backoff_rate=2)
     def set_worker(self):
         worker = self.worker
 
